(7. A database is a store for global variables) Tj
T*
(Source: seangoedecke.com RSS feed | Published: Fri, 06 Nov 2026 00:00:00 GMT) Tj
T*
(Link: https://seangoedecke.com/a-database-is-a-store-for-global-variables/) Tj
T*
(Databases are for storing global variables. We usually call them ?rows? or ?records? instead,) Tj
T*
(but they?re definitionally global variables: any part of your program can read or write them.) Tj
T*
(Isn?t this kind of weird? It?s common programming advice that global variables are bad, and yet) Tj
T*
(many elegant programs rely on databases. Either the common advice is wrong, or there?s) Tj
T*
(something special about databases that makes them the exception to the rule. What?s so bad) Tj
T*
(about global variables? Here?s the two main 1 reasons, in my view: They allow any part of your) Tj
T*
(program to implicitly send data to any other part instead of passing it around via function) Tj
T*
(signatures They couple different parts of your program: if one part depends on a variable) Tj
T*
(having some value, any other part can break it Both of these apply to databases. Any part of) Tj
T*
(your program that makes a query can send and receive data from any other part of your program.) Tj
T*
(In practice this happens all the time: one function will save some data on a record, and) Tj
T*
(another one will check for it. And if you haven?t seen bugs where some piece of code makes some) Tj
T*
(incorrect expectation about the shape 2 of a database column, you haven?t been working...) Tj
T*
